00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:00.160
But what's really
under the hood?

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.650
And of course, the
story starts with Otto.

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.520
Now who was Otto?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.590
Otto Von Guericke.

00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:11.980
Otto Von Guericke in the 1600s.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.270
He was like-- you know what--

00:02:13.270 --> 00:02:14.860
Aristotle.

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:19.440
Aristotle, he was all about
like nature abhors a vacuum.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:20.500
Does it really?

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:21.310
I don't know.

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:25.000
I don't-- you know,
does nature really hate?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.100
Like, let's try some stuff out.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.140
Let's try to make a vacuum,
and see what happens.

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.470
So he figured out a
way to take these two

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.540
hemispheres, these
two big bowls,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.140
and kind of pull
all the air out.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:36.640
There he is.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.100
He looks very happy there.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.360
Right.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.430
And he's pulling-- because
he's pumping the air out inside

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.240
of here, and look he did.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.050
He went around the
countryside in Germany,

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.850
and he would attach--
gesundheit-- just attach eight

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:51.160
horses to one side, and
eight horses to another,

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:53.633
16 horses pulling.

00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:55.300
Eight that way, eight
that way, and they

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.590
couldn't pull it apart.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:58.990
This is what he did.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.330
It doesn't look like
he's got a lot of crowd,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.980
but maybe he did better.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.320
I don't see any houses either.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.650
I don't know where he is.

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.650
But that's the experiment
that he did all

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.780
throughout the countryside.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:14.560
And he called it the
power of the vacuum.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.620
Nature abhors a vacuum,
the power of the vacuum.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:19.470
Power of the vacuum.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:21.160
It's not the power
of the vacuum.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.180
Oh, Otto, it's not the
power of the vacuum,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.480
because vacuum has no power,
and nature doesn't hate it.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:36.590
But pressure-- so
atmosphere has power.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:38.540
Technically, it has force.

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:39.680
Atmosphere has pressure.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.430
What he showed in
that experiment,

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:44.090
and if there's one
experiment I want

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:47.713
you to remember from this
class, it's not this one.

00:03:47.713 --> 00:03:48.680
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.410
But this is a cool one.

00:03:51.410 --> 00:03:53.480
But what he showed
wasn't the power vacuum,

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.840
he showed the power of one
atmosphere of pressure.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.240
He showed the power of pressure.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.460
And so we start.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.120
Pressure is force over area.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:09.620
Typical units would be like
newtons per meter squared,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:11.690
right.

00:04:11.690 --> 00:04:13.890
And if you're going
newtons per meter squared,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.860
then this is called the pascal.

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:22.670
And for reference, if you
have one pascal, one pascal,

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:30.260
it's about the pressure of
a dollar bill, $1 resting

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:31.338
on a table.

00:04:31.338 --> 00:04:31.880
I don't know.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:33.020
Why do I use that one?

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:37.400
Because I remember it,
$1 resting on a table.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:41.040
And so that's the force per area
of the bill resting on a table.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:45.080
So if you had 100,000
of them, right,

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:47.820
if you had 100,000 of them,
and you stacked them up,

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.960
then that's one atmosphere.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:52.420
That's just a different unit.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.140
It's about 100,000 pascals.

00:04:55.140 --> 00:05:01.540
So the power that he was
exhibiting in this experiment

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.830
is the power of the atmosphere.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:12.020
We've got-- we've got atmosphere
all around us, pushing on us.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:13.080
It's pushing on us.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:17.000
And so, we got like a
car worth of atmosphere,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:19.073
literally, on us, but
it's pushing on us

00:05:19.073 --> 00:05:20.240
in all different directions.

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:21.860
And we're pushing back.

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:25.820
Luckily, we've got
stuff inside of us,

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:27.980
so we don't like implode.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:29.460
Right.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.290
That force is strong, and that's
a demonstration of how strong,

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:36.790
how much one atmosphere is.

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:37.760
Right.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.910
It's force per area, the
power of one atmosphere.

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:43.160
Well, that's also directly
related to boiling.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.140
And how do we get
from here to boiling?

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:48.110
Well, so we need a couple
of concepts, right.

00:05:48.110 --> 00:05:53.180
So first, we need the
concept of evaporation.

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.320
And I'm going to use this--

00:05:55.320 --> 00:05:57.950
I'm going to use this example
of like water molecules, right.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:05:58.910
So this is a--

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:01.010
this is a glass of water.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:01.610
Right.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:04.418
And so, if I have
a glass of water,

00:06:04.418 --> 00:06:06.710
I'm going to draw them like
these little things-- yeah,

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.930
and now molecules kind
of near the surface--

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.860
well, it could leave.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.080
And that is-- that's a molecule,
not a v, right, and that's

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.930
evaporation, evaporation.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:20.120
Evaporation.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.980
But, you know, the
other way you could go

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.740
is, you could say, well, what
if there was this liquid here,

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:27.380
and you've got these
molecules, kind of like that.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:28.880
And you had one here.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.320
And it kind of comes back.

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:31.850
Well, that's condensation.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:33.440
You guys kind of
know this, right?

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:34.390
Condensation.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:39.350
Evaporation and condensation.

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:41.660
Oh, but this is not
what's interesting.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:43.790
I mean it's interesting,
but it's-- but it's more

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:46.120
