00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.430
One atmosphere.

00:14:13.430 --> 00:14:16.100
Well, you know, now
you see, like, OK,

00:14:16.100 --> 00:14:19.520
if a molecule is able to
evaporate from the liquid,

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:22.490
if a molecule is able to
leave the liquid bonding

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:25.370
environment, breaking
the bond, that's

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.760
why we can relate it to
intermolecular forces, right.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.770
It's breaking those
bonds in the liquid,

00:14:30.770 --> 00:14:32.360
and becoming a gas
phase molecule.

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:34.340
It's changing its phase.

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:35.810
Well, you know,
then the question

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.910
is, is it like going to--
what's it going to do?

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:41.090
You know, is it going to
kind of hang out here?

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:43.400
And are the forces
of the atmosphere,

00:14:43.400 --> 00:14:46.010
is the atmospheric pressure
going to kind of hold it

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:47.540
around there?

00:14:47.540 --> 00:14:52.340
Or, is it going to
be able to leave?

00:14:52.340 --> 00:14:55.170
Well, this is a very
simple conceptual picture.

00:14:55.170 --> 00:14:56.430
Right.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.430
If you thought that the
forces of the atmosphere

00:14:58.430 --> 00:14:59.888
were going to always
hold it there,

00:14:59.888 --> 00:15:02.860
then you'd say the
atmosphere is a container.

00:15:02.860 --> 00:15:03.680
And it's not.

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:05.510
But, actually,
evaporation only happens

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:08.940
because a breeze can come
along and blow this away--

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.280
right-- out in the air.

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:13.720
So those forces are
super important,

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:17.250
because as soon as the
vapor pressure is greater

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:19.530
than the atmospheric
pressure, well,

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.110
now you can get a sense of
what-- the vapor pressure is

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.100
greater than the pressure
outside over that surface

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:28.890
of the air here, then
the molecule is just

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.470
going to fly right out
and power through it.

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.015
That is the
definition of boiling.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.340
Boiling is a pressure effect.

00:15:38.340 --> 00:15:42.720
It's that the vapor pressure
of this material, which

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:46.620
is related to how strongly it's
bonded in the liquid compared

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:50.070
to the gas phase, that that
goes higher than the surrounding

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:51.270
pressure.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.340
That's the definition
of boiling.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.770
So you can see why
water doesn't boil.

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:00.740
Like having a bucket of
