00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:00.870
So how do you--

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:02.610
how does this get connected?

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:04.050
That's really the question.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:06.930
What is-- so what is this?

00:20:06.930 --> 00:20:07.440
Right.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.750
Where does that-- where does
that dependence come from?

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.280
And that's where
we need something

00:20:11.280 --> 00:20:12.360
called kinetic theory.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:14.828
Now I'm not going to
teach you kinetic theory.

00:20:14.828 --> 00:20:17.370
And I'm not going to say that
you responsible for knowing it.

00:20:17.370 --> 00:20:20.045
But I'm showing
you what it means.

00:20:20.045 --> 00:20:22.170
What kinetic theory does,
which you would learn in,

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.490
say like a thermal class, right.

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:27.180
Well, kinetic theory
does is it gives us

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.660
this relationship between how
many molecules have a given

00:20:30.660 --> 00:20:33.690
energy, in this case, it's
a kinetic energy, right,

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:35.340
and the temperature.

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:37.200
That's what can in
theory does for us.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:39.190
So let's see, if I had--

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:50.270
if I had to write this in a kind
of just simple dependence way,

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:53.665
what it's telling
us, in, general is--

00:20:53.665 --> 00:20:55.040
so this would be
kinetic theory--

00:20:57.900 --> 00:20:59.670
kinetic theory of
gases, if you want.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:02.210
That's what we're
talking about there.

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:03.810
OK.

00:21:03.810 --> 00:21:05.550
This would give
us the following.

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:11.890
This is what you get is that
the log of the fractions--

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:24.110
fraction of molecules with some
energy, E, OK, with energy--

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:26.940
I'll write it out just so
there's not confusion-- energy,

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:30.900
E, the log of that, the
natural log of that,

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:34.710
is going to go as that energy
divided by temperature.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:37.298
It gives you a
connection between what

00:21:37.298 --> 00:21:38.340
I'm plotting here really.

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:40.640
That's where that
plot comes from.

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:41.160
OK.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:43.890
The fraction of molecules
with kinetic energy,

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:49.150
E, goes as that energy
divided by temperature.

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:50.200
The log of that.

00:21:50.200 --> 00:21:51.850
That's what kinetic
theory gives me.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:54.250
That fills it in,
except that I--

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.480
it still isn't vapor pressure.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:56.980
Right.

00:21:56.980 --> 00:21:58.420
This still isn't vapor pressure.

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:03.220
And so for that, you
need one more piece.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:07.210
And that is given to us
by Clausius-Clapeyron.

00:22:07.210 --> 00:22:12.312
So the Clausius-Clapeyron
equation--

00:22:12.312 --> 00:22:14.740
ah-- Clapeyron-- does not--

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.820
I'm not going to derive it.

00:22:16.820 --> 00:22:19.270
You don't need to
know how to derive it.

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:22.540
But it goes from the kinetic
theory to vapor pressures.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:28.640
So this one tells us that
the log of the vapor pressure

00:22:28.640 --> 00:22:30.140
equals minus--

00:22:30.140 --> 00:22:34.430
and here we go-- this is the
next really important concept--

00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:36.765
the heat of vaporization--

00:22:36.765 --> 00:22:38.510
and we'll get to
this in a second--

00:22:38.510 --> 00:22:42.060
divided by RT plus a constant.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:44.300
So that's the
ideal gas constant.

00:22:44.300 --> 00:22:45.720
That's the temperature.

00:22:45.720 --> 00:22:50.040
And this is the enthalpy or
the heat of vaporization.

00:22:50.040 --> 00:22:59.670
So it's the energy
to vaporize a liquid.

00:22:59.670 --> 00:23:00.780
Good.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.180
Now, often, this is
given in per mole.

00:23:04.180 --> 00:23:06.280
So it would be like the
energy of vaporizing

00:23:06.280 --> 00:23:08.250
a mole of a liquid,
but it's an energy.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:11.280
It's an amount of energy
needed to turn it,

00:23:11.280 --> 00:23:13.110
whatever you got to
put into it, to turn it

00:23:13.110 --> 00:23:15.630
into a gas from a liquid.

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:17.190
That's that enthalpy
of vaporization.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:19.290
And R is the ideal gas
constant, in this case.

00:23:19.290 --> 00:23:22.140
You know, so if it's R--

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:25.470
if R is in the equation,
it's got to be per mole,

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:28.890
right, because it's
R. Remember, we've

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:30.090
already talked about that.

00:23:30.090 --> 00:23:31.900
Since it's R, it's the
ideal gas constant.

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:37.860
So that's, now, OK, so that
is how you get between--

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.510
that is how you get
this relationship.

00:23:39.510 --> 00:23:43.110
You get it, and, again, this
is an important equation.

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:45.750
You get it from the
kinetic theory gases,

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:47.970
but this is what the
outcome is for us.

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:52.000
This is what matters, is the
Clausius-Clapeyron equation.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:55.230
And so, and so now,
you can actually now,

00:23:55.230 --> 00:23:57.540
if somehow you knew
how much energy it

00:23:57.540 --> 00:24:00.030
took to vaporize a
mole of this stuff,

00:24:00.030 --> 00:24:02.130
well, now you've got a curve.

00:24:02.130 --> 00:24:05.070
That's all I need to know,
right, is how much energy is--

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:08.250
so now, I can say, well,
let's see, that's OK--

00:24:08.250 --> 00:24:14.470
I got-- I got, maybe butane
is going to go like this.

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:18.120
And then glycerol
might go like that.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.030
Maybe this is propanol.

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.240
Right.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:25.330
Propane, glycerol, and so on.

00:24:25.330 --> 00:24:25.830
Yeah.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.230
But I'm still
missing this concept.

00:24:28.230 --> 00:24:32.280
So I can now get dependence,
but how do I get delta H?

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.030
That's the next
piece of the puzzle.

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:34.530
Right.

00:24:34.530 --> 00:24:36.860
How do I get delta H?

00:24:36.860 --> 00:24:37.620
OK.

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:41.590
So, you know, so far I've got
this really cool result, right.

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:44.370
I've got that P--

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:46.420
so far I've got that--

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:48.910
I'm going back to bowling--

00:24:48.910 --> 00:24:50.170
I'm going back to boiling.

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:50.670
Right.

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:56.250
For boiling, I know that--

00:24:56.250 --> 00:25:02.790
so the ion-- so P vapor is
related to the IMFs, right.

00:25:02.790 --> 00:25:08.520
I know that P vapor is
related to the temperature.

00:25:08.520 --> 00:25:14.470
And I know that the atmospheric
pressure is important.

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:14.970
Right.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:19.150
Atmospheric pressure
is related to boiling.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:19.650
Yeah.

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:19.860
OK.

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:21.550
That's redundant,
because you've got

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:23.342
to get higher than the
atmospheric pressure

00:25:23.342 --> 00:25:24.120
to boiling.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:26.400
That's how we defined it.

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:30.090
So, but, again, this
specific dependence

00:25:30.090 --> 00:25:32.160
is what I wanted on
temperature, and I have it,

00:25:32.160 --> 00:25:34.740
except that I now need to
know what this delta H is.

00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:37.020
OK.

00:25:37.020 --> 00:25:40.650
So here we go-- why is
this related to pizza?

00:25:40.650 --> 00:25:43.470
Everything's related to
pizza at the end of the day.

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:47.250
But see, now we need
to talk about delta H,

00:25:47.250 --> 00:25:49.950
and what it means.

00:25:49.950 --> 00:25:52.920
And you know, the way--

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:55.140
the easiest way to
show what it means

00:25:55.140 --> 00:25:58.560
is to plot how this
relates to phase change.

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:02.830
How heat, right, that's how
we're changing phase here

00:26:02.830 --> 00:26:03.970
with temperatures.

00:26:03.970 --> 00:26:05.350
We're changing the temperature.

00:26:05.350 --> 00:26:08.190
We're adding heat
into the system.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:09.430
What does it do?

00:26:09.430 --> 00:26:10.460
What does it do?

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:13.630
Well, you know, so one type of
plot that you can make for that

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:22.600
would be temperature versus
thermal, thermal energy input.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:24.580
Now a lot of times what
you'll see in textbooks

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:26.560
is they call that q.

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:27.920
It's an energy term.

00:26:27.920 --> 00:26:29.430
So it's like in joules, right.

00:26:29.430 --> 00:26:29.972
Let's say.

00:26:29.972 --> 00:26:31.930
But it's how much thermal
energy you put it in,

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:33.170
then you can draw
it out like this.

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:33.670
Right.

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:37.450
So if you draw it out, then
you would have your phases.

00:26:37.450 --> 00:26:42.070
So this would be like a gas,
a liquid, a solid, and these

00:26:42.070 --> 00:26:43.060
would be phase changes.

00:26:46.420 --> 00:26:48.550
This, and that.

00:26:48.550 --> 00:26:52.240
So I'm adding thermal
energy into the system.

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:54.050
It's raising the
temperature of the system.

00:26:54.050 --> 00:26:55.507
That's why these are going up.

00:26:55.507 --> 00:26:58.090
But the solid is getting hotter,
and then, all of a sudden, it

00:26:58.090 --> 00:26:58.420
melts.

00:26:58.420 --> 00:26:58.690
OK.

00:26:58.690 --> 00:26:58.960
Good.

00:26:58.960 --> 00:26:59.590
So it melted.

00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:02.770
It melts.

00:27:02.770 --> 00:27:06.643
And then it's a liquid, and,
all of a sudden, it vaporizes.

00:27:06.643 --> 00:27:07.910
Right.

00:27:07.910 --> 00:27:08.760
Vaporize.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.390
But you can go the other way.

00:27:10.390 --> 00:27:13.040
If you go the other way,
it's condensation out here.

00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:13.540
All right.

00:27:13.540 --> 00:27:15.250
Or freezing in here.

00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:17.780
Melting, freezing,
vaporization, condensation.

00:27:17.780 --> 00:27:20.290
But I like to draw it this
way, because this way we're

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:23.470
going to see very
nicely what delta H is.

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:25.960
So here's-- now I'm going
to draw it with temperature

00:27:25.960 --> 00:27:27.110
on the x-axis.

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:31.900
So temperature-- and
this is going to be H.

00:27:31.900 --> 00:27:34.285
And I'm drawing this
as the thermal energy--

00:27:34.285 --> 00:27:36.570
let's see, I'm going to
write it in the same terms--

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:37.070
right.

00:27:37.070 --> 00:27:38.530
So this is thermal energy.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:44.260
Now in this case,
thermal energy,

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:48.760
we use this thing called
enthalpy, enthalpy.

00:27:48.760 --> 00:27:52.837
But you don't need to worry too
much about it, for this class,

00:27:52.837 --> 00:27:54.670
you can think about it
as the thermal energy

00:27:54.670 --> 00:27:56.780
content of the material.

00:27:56.780 --> 00:27:58.390
So if I have a change
in that, wells,

00:27:58.390 --> 00:28:01.460
it's because I've lost
or gained thermal energy.

00:28:01.460 --> 00:28:02.290
Right.

00:28:02.290 --> 00:28:03.910
If you take a
thermal class, you'll

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:06.220
learn a lot more about enthalpy.

00:28:06.220 --> 00:28:08.560
And that this is true only
if you have constant pressure

00:28:08.560 --> 00:28:10.510
conditions, but that's
not relevant here.

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:14.360
I just want you to
know, this is like this.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:15.050
OK.

00:28:15.050 --> 00:28:19.660
But this is the total thermal
energy in your material.

00:28:19.660 --> 00:28:27.620
And by the way, you know,
q, heat, the word heat is q.

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:32.030
That is the definite-- heat
means thermal energy transfer.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:35.750
Thermal energy transfer,
a flow of heat.

00:28:35.750 --> 00:28:36.980
That makes sense.

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:40.280
But you cannot say that
this has 10 joules of heat.

00:28:40.280 --> 00:28:42.012
No, don't say that.

00:28:42.012 --> 00:28:43.970
You will sound like you're
from another school.

00:28:43.970 --> 00:28:46.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:50.330
You don't have-- you
have that much enthalpy.

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:50.900
Yeah.

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:51.770
But not that much.

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:54.500
Heat is a transfer of
thermal energy, please.

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:57.590
OK.

00:28:57.590 --> 00:28:58.860
Now here we go.

00:28:58.860 --> 00:28:59.360
All right.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:01.417
So let's suppose I
were to draw this.

00:29:01.417 --> 00:29:03.000
Well, now I'm going
to have the same--

00:29:03.000 --> 00:29:04.250
I'm going to have phases.

00:29:04.250 --> 00:29:05.120
Right.

00:29:05.120 --> 00:29:08.240
And so here we go,
here we go, here we go.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:11.570
And this is my gas,
and this is my liquid,

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:13.215
and this is my solid.

00:29:13.215 --> 00:29:14.840
There's a couple of
cool things, right.

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:18.020
So now-- so that's
the thermal energy, H.

00:29:18.020 --> 00:29:20.420
But so now, if I
were to take part

00:29:20.420 --> 00:29:24.170
of the solid like that, and just
look at it, while it's a solid.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:27.350
I'm adding thermal energy to it.

00:29:27.350 --> 00:29:29.060
I'm increasing its temperature.

00:29:29.060 --> 00:29:30.560
We got a way to
relate those things.

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:32.780
It's called the heat capacity.

00:29:32.780 --> 00:29:34.040
All right.

00:29:34.040 --> 00:29:36.330
It's called the heat capacity.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:41.660
So if I look at that,
OK, I can do that.

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:43.090
Let's go here.

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:43.850
All right.

00:29:43.850 --> 00:29:47.090
If I look at that, then
I can relate those by--

00:29:47.090 --> 00:29:49.790
well, I'm using q, because
it's a change, right, that's

00:29:49.790 --> 00:29:51.690
the change in the enthalpy.

00:29:51.690 --> 00:29:52.190
All right.

00:29:52.190 --> 00:29:54.080
That's the energy transfer.

00:29:54.080 --> 00:29:56.960
It goes as the heat
capacity times the change

00:29:56.960 --> 00:29:58.160
in the temperature.

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:01.160
That s just means it's the
heat capacity of the solid.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:05.480
Literally, that's the
definition of heat capacity.

00:30:05.480 --> 00:30:07.160
Right.

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:10.250
This would be like units
of joules per kelvin.

00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:11.780
OK.

00:30:11.780 --> 00:30:15.390
So and, example of heat
capacity, I love pizza,

00:30:15.390 --> 00:30:18.980
and so an example of heat
capacity is the following.

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:21.500
You burn, hopefully, you don't.

00:30:21.500 --> 00:30:24.050
But if you take it out of the
oven, and you take a bite,

00:30:24.050 --> 00:30:26.330
right away, you will burn
the roof of your mouth

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:28.060
on the cheese.

00:30:28.060 --> 00:30:31.660
On the cheese, because cheese
has a lot of water in it

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:33.010
compared to the crust.

00:30:33.010 --> 00:30:37.280
And water has a really
high heat capacity.

00:30:37.280 --> 00:30:38.360
What is heat capacity?

00:30:38.360 --> 00:30:42.560
It's the capacity of it
to have thermal energy.

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:45.710
And the water has
a really high one,

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:48.500
and so, when you take
this out, everything's

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.360
at the same temperature
for just an instant.

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:51.980
They're all at the
same temperature.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:55.282
If you eat it right
here, please don't, you

00:30:55.282 --> 00:30:57.740
burn your mouth on the crust
the same way as on the cheese.

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.420
But since you're
waiting, now the crust

00:30:59.420 --> 00:31:01.378
gives out-- it doesn't
have much heat capacity.

00:31:01.378 --> 00:31:02.570
So it all goes away.

00:31:02.570 --> 00:31:05.360
All that thermal energy
leaves very quickly

00:31:05.360 --> 00:31:06.890
from the crust,
but not the cheese,

00:31:06.890 --> 00:31:07.910
because it's got so much more.

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:09.650
And if we want to get a
little more serious about it,

00:31:09.650 --> 00:31:11.210
we can talk about the oceans.

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:12.470
And so, I put this in here.

00:31:12.470 --> 00:31:14.870
Because we talk a lot
about climate change, what

00:31:14.870 --> 00:31:18.050
you're talking about are the
temperatures of the atmosphere.

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:19.620
We talk about that a lot.

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.050
But what gets sort
of shoved aside

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:25.910
is the temperature of the
ocean, and that is rising,

00:31:25.910 --> 00:31:27.950
and it's not rising
that quickly, luckily,

00:31:27.950 --> 00:31:30.830
but that's because the ocean
has such a high heat capacity.

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:34.670
That's where most of this
thermal energy is going.

00:31:34.670 --> 00:31:37.430
It's going into the ocean.

00:31:37.430 --> 00:31:37.930
Right.

00:31:37.930 --> 00:31:39.555
And we need to start
talking about that

00:31:39.555 --> 00:31:44.600
a whole lot more, because it's
a lot higher than it used to be.

00:31:44.600 --> 00:31:48.620
This is the historical
reference, pretty flat.

00:31:48.620 --> 00:31:53.940
A volcano erupts, 500 years
ago, you get a blip this big.

00:31:53.940 --> 00:31:55.030
Now we're here.

00:31:55.030 --> 00:31:55.530
Right.

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:58.590
So the amount of thermal energy
this ocean is holding for us

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:00.600
is very high.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:01.140
OK.

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:02.340
Now so that's high capacity.

00:32:02.340 --> 00:32:04.430
That's kind of an
important part,

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:07.030
but, look, the other
thing we have here-- ah,

00:32:07.030 --> 00:32:08.928
I keep hitting the
wrong-- is this.

00:32:08.928 --> 00:32:10.470
And that's what I
want to talk about.

00:32:10.470 --> 00:32:14.820
This is that delta
H. Now in this case,

00:32:14.820 --> 00:32:18.180
it's the change in
energy of fusion,

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.010
because you're fusing
to make a solid.

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:23.066
In this case, it's called
delta H of vaporization.

00:32:23.066 --> 00:32:27.606
You could call it
condensation vaporization.

00:32:31.940 --> 00:32:33.230
That's the thing we needed.

00:32:33.230 --> 00:32:34.920
And now you see
it so beautifully.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:35.420
All right.

00:32:35.420 --> 00:32:41.060
You see it so beautifully right
there, because, now, you see--

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:45.140
so what is it that causes me
to need to put a lot of energy

00:32:45.140 --> 00:32:49.730
into a material to change its
bonds so that it can vaporize?

00:32:49.730 --> 00:32:52.310
It's all about the
bonding strength.

00:32:52.310 --> 00:32:53.300
It's all about the--

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:55.080
everything comes to
the bonding strength.

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:55.580
Right.

00:32:55.580 --> 00:32:57.500
And so I have these kind
of two things, right.

00:32:57.500 --> 00:33:02.360
Within one phase,
within one phase,

00:33:02.360 --> 00:33:08.990
I can relate delta H to delta T.
And that relationship is here.

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:10.370
I'm in the solid phase.

00:33:10.370 --> 00:33:11.430
I can relate them.

00:33:11.430 --> 00:33:14.570
That's actually
called sensible heat.

00:33:14.570 --> 00:33:17.145
Sensible heat.

00:33:17.145 --> 00:33:19.395
It's the energy that you
would give off when you cool,

00:33:19.395 --> 00:33:22.370
or it's the energy that
you'd absorb when you heat.

00:33:22.370 --> 00:33:24.630
But now I've got
this other thing,

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:28.180
which is at the phase
change, at the phase change.

00:33:28.180 --> 00:33:29.930
Well, now, the temperature
doesn't change,

00:33:29.930 --> 00:33:31.850
but I'm putting
all of this energy

00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:34.010
in, or getting it all out.

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:35.910
That's what delta H is.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:36.410
All right.

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:40.430
So delta T is 0, because
the temperature is constant.

00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:42.950
And delta H is
sort of, generally,

00:33:42.950 --> 00:33:46.375
called the latent
heat, latent heat.

00:33:46.375 --> 00:33:47.750
If you're talking
about the phase

00:33:47.750 --> 00:33:49.167
change between a
solid and liquid,

00:33:49.167 --> 00:33:51.860
it's the heat of
fusion, heat of melting.

00:33:51.860 --> 00:33:55.415
If you're talking about
between the liquid and gas

00:33:55.415 --> 00:33:56.665
vaporization, or condensation.

00:33:56.665 --> 00:33:58.200
So that's that energy change.

00:33:58.200 --> 00:34:02.190
And it's related to, well,
what we've been talking about,

00:34:02.190 --> 00:34:03.800
but here it isn't
a nice diagram.

00:34:03.800 --> 00:34:04.300
Right.

00:34:04.300 --> 00:34:07.350
Now, where do we go from here?

00:34:07.350 --> 00:34:09.350
Well, there's something
so cool about this graph

00:34:09.350 --> 00:34:11.699
that I can show
you really easily.

00:34:11.699 --> 00:34:13.830
I can also tell
you how to do this,

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:15.440
which is that things can--

00:34:15.440 --> 00:34:18.320
these are the phase change
transition temperatures,

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:20.389
but things don't need
to transition at those.

00:34:20.389 --> 00:34:24.400
In fact, oftentimes, you
might get something like that.

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:26.360
Not as much in the gas,
but liquid to solid,

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:27.650
you certainly can get it.

00:34:27.650 --> 00:34:30.530
This is called super cooled.

00:34:30.530 --> 00:34:31.190
Why?

00:34:31.190 --> 00:34:35.780
Because, for some reason,
[INAUDIBLE],, for some reason,

00:34:35.780 --> 00:34:39.800
it's remained a liquid below
its phase change temperature.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:42.020
Here's water, super cooled,
poured into a glass.

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:45.020
It's one of the most fun
things you can do in life.

00:34:45.020 --> 00:34:46.100
There it is.

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:47.213
It's a liquid.

00:34:47.213 --> 00:34:49.505
As soon as it hits the glass,
it's like, wait a second,

00:34:49.505 --> 00:34:51.530
I want to be a solid.

00:34:51.530 --> 00:34:53.650
I'm over here.

00:34:53.650 --> 00:34:54.520
I'm over here.

00:34:54.520 --> 00:34:56.620
I need to be a solid.

00:34:56.620 --> 00:34:58.000
And you can do this.

00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:58.720
You can make--

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:01.000
I can tell you how to make it.

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:03.430
You can make super
cooled water at home.

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:04.580
It's really fun.

00:35:04.580 --> 00:35:06.910
But that was what you're
doing there, now you know,

00:35:06.910 --> 00:35:10.553
is you're bringing this
down below the phase change.

00:35:10.553 --> 00:35:11.470
It's remaining liquid.

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:13.095
Now all of a sudden,
it hits the glass,

00:35:13.095 --> 00:35:14.380
and, but what am I doing?

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:16.160
I need to solidify.

00:35:16.160 --> 00:35:17.140
All right.

00:35:17.140 --> 00:35:17.740
OK.

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:21.570
This is the reason why all this
matters is heat is a big deal.

00:35:21.570 --> 00:35:24.550
90%, you now, this is--
you can study this.

00:35:24.550 --> 00:35:26.170
There are wonderful
charts to study.

00:35:26.170 --> 00:35:28.960
But depending on
what you want to do,

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:30.970
heat is almost always involved.

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:34.510
Heat being a transfer
of thermal energy.

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:37.210
And so 90% of the
current energy budget,

00:35:37.210 --> 00:35:40.860
basically, goes through
the middle in some way.

00:35:40.860 --> 00:35:42.690
That's a lot.

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:44.190
But then, if you
look at things like

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:47.660
this, which you shouldn't,
because you can't read it,

00:35:47.660 --> 00:35:48.540
but if you blow up--

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:50.850
these are all the
inputs on the left,

00:35:50.850 --> 00:35:52.770
all the ways we make energy.

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:54.000
Here's how we use it.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.690
And here's the result.

00:35:55.690 --> 00:35:59.340
Here's where I want to get the
punch line is, rejected energy.

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:03.060
Whatever these units,
doesn't matter, it's 60%.

00:36:03.060 --> 00:36:04.540
What is rejected energy mean?

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:05.860
Wasted.

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:08.080
All this precious fossil
fuel stuff we're burning

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:09.830
and all the solar
energy we're collecting,

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:13.570
anything we're doing here,
60% of it goes into heat,

00:36:13.570 --> 00:36:15.540
wasted heat.

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:17.530
And so the reason
why this matters

00:36:17.530 --> 00:36:19.360
is because there
are these materials

00:36:19.360 --> 00:36:22.340
that we can use to try
to capture some of that.

00:36:22.340 --> 00:36:22.840
Right.

00:36:22.840 --> 00:36:25.070
There are materials we can use
to try to capture some of that,

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:26.530
and it all comes
down to chemistry.

00:36:26.530 --> 00:36:29.420
These are called phase
change materials.

00:36:29.420 --> 00:36:32.856
And if you plot the melting
temperature, that's this--

00:36:32.856 --> 00:36:35.860
that's this melting
temperature, right, Tm,

00:36:35.860 --> 00:36:41.260
versus how much of its delta
H, how much delta H you have,

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:43.600
you get all sorts of
classes and materials.

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:46.270
But, you know, so you might want
a certain melting temperature

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:48.520
from wherever you're operating,
but then you might not

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:50.622
get a high enough
delta H, or you

00:36:50.622 --> 00:36:53.080
might want really high delta
Hs, but then the thing doesn't

00:36:53.080 --> 00:36:55.820
melt until 700c,
which is way too high.

00:36:55.820 --> 00:36:57.732
So we got to fill this out.

00:36:57.732 --> 00:36:58.690
We need materials here.

00:36:58.690 --> 00:36:59.648
We need materials here.

00:36:59.648 --> 00:37:01.910
That's a call to chemistry.

00:37:01.910 --> 00:37:03.680
That's a call to chemistry.

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:06.470
So then, it all has to
do with the phase change.

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:07.310
It's a weird name.

00:37:07.310 --> 00:37:09.720
These are called phase
change materials,

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:12.530
but that's true
of all materials.

00:37:12.530 --> 00:37:15.650
But, here, when you're talking
about storing thermal energy,

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:17.990
we use the term phase
change materials.

00:37:17.990 --> 00:37:18.770
OK.

00:37:18.770 --> 00:37:22.520
Now the last concept, and
I'll just touch on, and then

00:37:22.520 --> 00:37:28.650
