00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:27.480
What happened is, you've
reached a temperature

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:32.070
where it, basically, can't
exist as a liquid anymore.

00:46:32.070 --> 00:46:36.530
If you keep going, the thermal
energy is just too high.

00:46:36.530 --> 00:46:38.760
But the pressure
is too high, too.

00:46:38.760 --> 00:46:41.470
So this is a different phase.

00:46:41.470 --> 00:46:44.730
And this phase it out here
is called supercritical.

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:46.860
This is called supercritical,
where the material

00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:49.880
has properties of both.

00:46:49.880 --> 00:46:50.880
That's really cool.

00:46:50.880 --> 00:46:51.380
Right.

00:46:51.380 --> 00:46:53.430
So this material
has maybe properties

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:56.220
like it can flow like
a gas, all right,

00:46:56.220 --> 00:46:58.140
but it's got a higher
density than a gas,

00:46:58.140 --> 00:47:01.170
but it's still
lighter than a liquid.

00:47:01.170 --> 00:47:02.990
And it's that same material.

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:05.760
It's that same material,
whatever it is, water.

00:47:05.760 --> 00:47:06.570
Right.

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:07.760
That's called supercritical.

00:47:07.760 --> 00:47:10.510
So these lines
can end like that.

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:12.690
And that's called
the critical point.

00:47:12.690 --> 00:47:15.570
Now, there, you know,
there are many reasons

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:17.280
why these maps are important.

00:47:17.280 --> 00:47:20.190
I thought I'd give you
one which is strawberries.

00:47:20.190 --> 00:47:22.740
Because, if you go and you buy--

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:25.275
some of us like to
buy like dried fruit--

00:47:25.275 --> 00:47:27.900
but if I take strawberries and
I leave them out on the counter,

00:47:27.900 --> 00:47:30.580
they're not going
to look like this.

00:47:30.580 --> 00:47:32.090
You know, how do you know that?

00:47:32.090 --> 00:47:33.882
Well, you know that
from the phase diagram,

00:47:33.882 --> 00:47:37.350
because if I draw my
temperature and pressure,

00:47:37.350 --> 00:47:42.900
and I've got some phase diagram
that looks like this, right.

00:47:42.900 --> 00:47:47.130
And so now, you know
solid, liquid, gas.

00:47:47.130 --> 00:47:48.870
Well, you know, I could--

00:47:48.870 --> 00:47:53.320
if I just-- if I just tried
to dry these things out,

00:47:53.320 --> 00:47:55.304
that's this.

00:47:55.304 --> 00:47:56.830
That's that.

00:47:56.830 --> 00:48:00.690
But so, I'm-- maybe, maybe, I'm
going to, or maybe I want to do

00:48:00.690 --> 00:48:01.190
this.

00:48:01.190 --> 00:48:02.820
Oh, that's even worse.

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:04.807
What if I go like this.

00:48:04.807 --> 00:48:06.390
I'm going to heat
the strawberries up.

00:48:06.390 --> 00:48:08.310
I'm making jam.

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:08.820
No.

00:48:08.820 --> 00:48:10.090
That's not jam.

00:48:10.090 --> 00:48:10.590
Right.

00:48:10.590 --> 00:48:13.020
That's nice kind of
maybe still solid,

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:16.110
it's not like mushy and gooey.

00:48:16.110 --> 00:48:17.300
How do I get it?

00:48:17.300 --> 00:48:19.680
Well, you use the phase
diagram, of course.

00:48:19.680 --> 00:48:23.730
You go-- because this
kind of phase change,

00:48:23.730 --> 00:48:25.860
going across that
phase boundary,

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:29.130
it creates a lot of
disruption in the material.

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:30.630
It creates a lot of disruption.

00:48:30.630 --> 00:48:32.740
You saw it, right, this boiling.

00:48:32.740 --> 00:48:36.180
So if you do that for the water
inside of the strawberries,

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:41.670
you're going to destroy all the
framework of the good stuff,

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:43.710
right.

00:48:43.710 --> 00:48:45.570
So what you do is you freeze it.

00:48:45.570 --> 00:48:49.290
You go this way, and
then you go that way.

00:48:49.290 --> 00:48:53.520
If you do that,
now the solid as it

00:48:53.520 --> 00:48:56.370
sublimates is much
less of a kind

00:48:56.370 --> 00:48:59.370
of violent,
disturbing phenomenon

00:48:59.370 --> 00:49:02.130
to the surrounding
matrix of strawberry.

00:49:02.130 --> 00:49:04.650
And so you get these freeze
dried fruit that way.

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:09.060
It's much less disturbing,
destructive way

00:49:09.060 --> 00:49:10.060
to get rid of the water.

00:49:10.060 --> 00:49:11.185
That's called freeze dried.

00:49:11.185 --> 00:49:13.630
You can also go the
other-- if I go out here,

00:49:13.630 --> 00:49:18.430
I've got all sorts of uses to
use this supercritical region.

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:21.490
I've got all sorts of ways
where that becomes useful.

00:49:21.490 --> 00:49:22.990
That's how coffee
is decaffeinated,

00:49:22.990 --> 00:49:25.280
because it's a different
phase of this material.

00:49:25.280 --> 00:49:27.580
So the phase maps give
us this information.

00:49:27.580 --> 00:49:32.080
They tell us how we can engineer
properties and materials

00:49:32.080 --> 00:49:33.840
by engineering their phases.

00:49:33.840 --> 00:49:35.370
Right.

00:49:35.370 --> 00:49:38.830
And if you pull out
another thing, you know--

00:49:38.830 --> 00:49:40.690
a lot of the textbooks
and what we did

00:49:40.690 --> 00:49:42.585
is we look at the phase
diagram down here.

00:49:42.585 --> 00:49:43.960
But if you pull
out, you also can

00:49:43.960 --> 00:49:46.100
see how complex materials are.

00:49:46.100 --> 00:49:46.600
All right.

00:49:46.600 --> 00:49:48.227
This is water.

00:49:48.227 --> 00:49:50.310
We've been focusing on
this part of phase diagram,

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:51.460
now you plot it.

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:57.900
Water has 17 phases, 17, not 3.

00:49:57.900 --> 00:50:00.360
And just two years ago,
the 17th was discovered,

00:50:00.360 --> 00:50:03.650
and it's a very, very
light form of ice.

00:50:03.650 --> 00:50:04.160
All right.

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:07.975
So you can get all sorts of
really important information

00:50:07.975 --> 00:50:10.100
about materials by looking
at their phase diagrams.

00:50:10.100 --> 00:50:11.600
You can compare
different materials,

00:50:11.600 --> 00:50:15.620
like this is the phase
diagram of CO2 versus water.

00:50:15.620 --> 00:50:17.900
And you can learn
about sort of where

00:50:17.900 --> 00:50:19.250
their critical points are.

00:50:19.250 --> 00:50:22.460
You can see all the
slope here in water is--

00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:24.830
most material slope like that.

00:50:24.830 --> 00:50:26.210
Water slopes like that.

00:50:26.210 --> 00:50:27.973
And that's because
of hydrogen bonds.

00:50:27.973 --> 00:50:29.390
Because the hydrogen
bonds make it

00:50:29.390 --> 00:50:33.980
so that the solid is less dense
than the liquid, which means,

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:36.920
if I squeeze on it, I can--

00:50:36.920 --> 00:50:38.510
then I go to the liquid.

00:50:38.510 --> 00:50:40.010
That's really weird.

00:50:40.010 --> 00:50:42.770
If I squeeze on
this, it's more soft.

00:50:42.770 --> 00:50:45.970
But here, it goes-- it can
cross a phase boundary.

00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:48.610
That's very unique to
water, and the fish

00:50:48.610 --> 00:50:52.390
are very happy about
that, because ice floats.

00:50:52.390 --> 00:50:53.190
OK.

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:56.590
Now, OK, now, so that's the
kind of last part of the phase

00:50:56.590 --> 00:50:58.470
diagram stuff.

